WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.763
What and who is onscreen is important.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.540
We're all coming to
appreciate that more and more,

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.180
especially given
everything we've witnessed

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.430
in the past couple of years.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.930
But what's just as
crucial, maybe more crucial

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.000
in the long run, is
who's behind the camera,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.053
and even more crucial than
that, who has the money.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.810
And that's where Charles D. King comes in.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.710
This good business that we're building

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.650
is absolutely gonna make the world better.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.010
The stories are being told
about a community that are

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.410
actually from a community
instead of a lens of being told

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:37.793
from someone else.

00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:40.780
Though King isn't
exactly a household name,

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.720
few people in the entertainment
industry have more influence

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.070
on what gets made,
watched and talked about.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.520
Black and other minority artists

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.610
have claimed a growing
share of mainstream power

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.300
and recognition over the
past couple of decades.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.970
King has often been
there behind the scenes,

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.630
making the right deals,
empowering fresh talent

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.180
and ultimately, starting
his own company centered

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.970
on creators of color.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.240
Charles is always like a voice

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.600
that reminds me to look at things

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.270
from a 10,000-foot view,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.570
where do you wanna be
in five years, 10 years?

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.170
And he automatically has
our ear and our trust.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.533
He's been very, very
instrumental in my life.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.790
I visited Charles at his
home in Beverly Hills.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.710
I wanted to find out how he made his way

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.760
from sorting mail at a talent agency

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.343
to reshaping the landscape
of entertainment.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.850
So let's start at the beginning if we can.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.814
Decatur, Georgia, the childhood,

00:01:39.814 --> 00:01:41.370
we're going all the way back.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.160
Look, I was born in Harlem,
and then my family moved south

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.040
when I was four.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.433
But then we landed in Atlanta
in the suburbs, Decatur.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.510
It was a nice, middle-class neighborhood,

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.490
and it was great.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.420
I mean, I grew up playing
soccer and tennis,

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.260
and my father's a pediatrician,

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.220
and I saw him build this thriving practice

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.380
and have his own own
business during that time.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.170
King went to college at
Vanderbilt University

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.380
in Nashville, lured by
a generous scholarship.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.630
The Tennessee capital is, of
course, a city of entertainers.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.900
The young King was drawn

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.480
to the business side of the industry,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.360
but he was also inspired
by a television show

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.710
whose casting foreshadowed his own work,

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.490
changing who was on the screen.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:25.670
This show that I used to watch

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.460
with my mother back in high
school called "LA Law,"

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.050
this light bulb went off in my head.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.700
This character played by Blair
Underwood, this charismatic,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.490
intelligent African-American man,

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.880
and I was like, "Oh, that
Blair Underwood character,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.220
that could be really cool
when I go to law school."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.380
My life experiences growing
up as a young black man

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.520
in our country, seeing
some challenges and issues

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.480
that we're dealing with, there
were some experiences I had

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.760
during that time that also
had an interest for me

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:50.940
in civil rights.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.020
And so I thought, "Lemme
go to Howard Law."

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.600
And yet, you probably could have,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.930
you could have been Blair Underwood

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.800
or the Blair Underwood
character in many ways

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.720
and gone to work for a big law firm

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.460
and sort of taken that path.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.890
It was clear for me too

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.540
that that wasn't the path for me.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.210
The summer after my second year,

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.530
I got a clerkship at MTV,
and one of the lawyers

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.650
that I worked with there
said, "Hey, Charles,

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.290
I think you would make
a really good lawyer,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.050
but you'd make a great agent."

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:21.010
And I had no idea about the agency world,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.250
and I learned about all
these moguls in the industry,

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.170
David Geffen and Barry Diller.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.417
Mike Ovitz had just come out of CAA

00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:30.830
and had this incredible gig
at Disney that didn't work,

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.720
and his one-year payout was 95 million.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.730
So I was like, "Wait a
minute, this agency world,"

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.280
and I thought that actually
could be a good fit for me.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.730
So King headed west, as so many have,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.660
in search of the future in Hollywood

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.363
with his then girlfriend
and now wife, Stacey.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.510
And also like many before and after him,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.543
fame and fortune were not
immediately forthcoming.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.860
So this is where it started.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.340
This is where I started
when I came to L.A.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.920
I said, "Lemme start at the
oldest entertainment business

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.230
in the industry," and at the time,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:03.640
it's the William Morris agency

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.190
and that legendary agent trainee program,

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.649
and they were located right
here in this building.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.794
I mean, you are at that point,

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:16.230
incredibly well-credentialed academically,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.857
and the way that the world worked

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:19.930
and it probably still works to some extent

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:21.510
is you gotta go work in the mail room.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:22.808
Yes.

00:04:22.808 --> 00:04:25.740
I mean, what does that
feel like at that moment?

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.090
It was tough.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.050
It was very challenging financially,

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.640
90 hours a week for very little pay.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.650
I had $100,000 in student loans.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.940
I had to defer them.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.800
There's a big reason why,
particularly at that time,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:40.700
there were not many people

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.290
from diverse backgrounds in there.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.550
One, it's hard to get in,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.170
but two, financially, it's
challenging to afford it.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.980
There were no African-American
trainees when I started.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.790
There were a few assistants
throughout the building.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.810
Let's say if there were
two or 300 assistants,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.890
maybe there were two.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.030
Wow.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.430
But it was no different at
any of the other agencies.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.170
That was just what the
shape of the town was.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:03.610
The analogy I used to use

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.080
of what it was like coming
up through the ranks,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.149
it's like working on the
floor of the stock exchange,

00:05:09.149 --> 00:05:11.690
ticker tape and people
yelling and screaming,

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.290
rolling phone calls, and you
got four or five lines going,

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:16.240
working with and representing at times,

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.200
I mean, up to maybe like 100 artists.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.750
So frankly, the threshold

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.720
of pain of literally
constantly putting out fires

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.210
and solving a problem, I can't think

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.423
of a better training ground
for what I'm doing now.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.970
King didn't stay in
the mail room for long,

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.000
quickly working his way up to assistant,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.390
then junior agent, just
as many had before.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.850
Where he went off-script, as it were,

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.840
was with his client list.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.400
Whether it was packaging
the hit movie "Barbershop"

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.000
or repping Andre 3000 and DMX,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.220
King was becoming a
kingmaker for black culture.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:53.440
I would share all of the
statistics and articles

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.920
about the shifting demographics
and where we're going

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.210
and that hip hop culture is youth culture,

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.590
and youth culture is pop culture,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.260
and everything is going in that direction,

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.120
and these are the
demographics that are growing

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.790
and that the same thing is gonna happen

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.140
in film and television.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.280
That point of view started
to look pretty prescient

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.550
in 2005 when a couple of
Charles's film clients

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:14.950
hit the big time.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:17.870
After talking about Tyler
Perry for years in meetings,

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.600
inviting people out to see his plays,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.460
we'd packaged his first movie,
"Diary of a Mad Black Woman"

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.170
and set up this unprecedented
deal for him at Lionsgate

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.170
to co-finance and own the movie.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:31.440
And that small film was a massive hit.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.110
It may like 50 million at box office,

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.150
and it sold, I dunno, a
ridiculous amount of DVDs,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.940
and it became the cornerstone foundation

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.610
of this incredible business
that Tyler has built.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.079
And so that happened in 2005.

00:06:43.079 --> 00:06:45.190
Craig Brewer was a young filmmaker

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.350
that I started representing
about a month before Sundance

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:50.250
that year, and he had this
movie called "Hustle and Flow."

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:52.446
Feel that.

00:06:52.446 --> 00:06:55.873
♪ You know it's hard out here for a pimp ♪

00:06:55.873 --> 00:06:57.800
"Hustle and Flow" went to Sundance,

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.200
and that film, made for 3 1/2 million,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.580
became the biggest sale
at Sundance that year.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.910
And then a few months after that,

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.340
we started representing Terrence
Howard who got nominated

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.300
for an Academy Award later on that year.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.990
And yeah, so I went from
regular agent in 2000

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:15.050
to the senior vice president by 2008

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.390
who was running our big
motion picture meetings.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:19.780
As you climb the ladder,
this is a good business.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.613
It is.
Absolutely.

00:07:20.613 --> 00:07:21.830
And you're representing some

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.320
of the biggest names in the world,

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:27.550
and yet, you say, "Yeah, but
I wanna do this other thing."

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.060
When I went into the mail
room, it was with a vision

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.240
of a 10-year plan to
one day be at the helm

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.760
of my own diversified
entertainment and media company.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.890
I definitely knew okay,
this vision that I have,

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.590
this is now the time for me to begin

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.923
to put the pieces in place.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.600
King left his job to start Macro in 2015.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.210
He was determined not only to create

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.240
a flourishing entertainment business,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.970
but to fulfill a social mission, as well,

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.410
centered around a radically

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.360
and historically underserved community

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.510
of creators and audiences.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.400
Over time, we created this
thing called the Macro Rubric,

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.080
and there's six key
points that we look for.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.350
At the center of every
one of the projects,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.990
people of color must
be at the center of it,

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.750
either the lead, co-lead,
the driving through-line,

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.620
and people of color on a broad sense,

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.220
African-American, Latinx, Asian,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.640
the wide different demographic
that I call the new majority.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.630
And so we choose projects,
one, have people of color

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.720
at the center of them,
projects that have a level

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.100
of excellence of storytelling.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.193
And the other thing we of course look for,

00:08:27.193 --> 00:08:28.060
I mean, all of the years experience,

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:28.960
is we're looking at what

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.840
is the business opportunity for them.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.540
We're gonna take some calculated risk,

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:35.670
but here's where the upside
opportunity is for it.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:39.010
His stature within entertainment,
the client list alone,

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.550
would seem to make starting Macro

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:43.230
a fairly low risk proposition.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:44.800
It wasn't.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.320
He and his wife, Stacey,
self-funded for six months

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.610
while investor after investor passed.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:54.340
It's not a slam dunk when you
go out to pitch it to people.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:55.173
Not at all.

00:08:55.173 --> 00:08:56.006
Were you surprised?

00:08:56.006 --> 00:08:57.440
It was harrowing.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:58.273
When I made the leap,

00:08:58.273 --> 00:08:59.860
I knew that there were
gonna be some roadblocks

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.550
and there might be some challenges,

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:04.270
but that if anyone had the
credentials and the background

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.630
to do it, that I stood
about as strong a shot

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.700
as anyone ever would up to that point.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.373
How come you ain't never liked me?

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:14.173
Like you?

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.623
Who the hell said I got to like you?

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.410
Macro's momentum really picked up

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.460
when they co-financed the
movie "Fences" in 2016.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:26.030
Starring Denzel Washington
and Viola Davis,

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.810
the adaptation of the August
Wilson play helped define

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.700
what Macro would be at its core,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.500
combining star power,
artistic seriousness,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.240
and commercial potential.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.965
And the Oscar goes to Viola Davis.

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.770
But Macro's projects,

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.660
with their largely Black-centric
stories and creators,

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.230
would continue to run into
challenges in the marketplace.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.770
One striking example would
be their highly acclaimed

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.550
gothic Southern drama, "Mudbound" in 2017.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.065
If we could look at the laptop.

00:10:01.065 --> 00:10:01.898
Yes.

00:10:01.898 --> 00:10:04.980
I want you to watch what
one of your pals said

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.040
about a very specific movie.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.740
Let's check it out.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.160
The pal in question:

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.450
Ted Sarandos, the CEO of
Netflix. Nice friend to have.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:18.860
Our first deal really we
did together was "Mudbound."

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:22.937
I was in Park City the last
couple of days of Sundance.

00:10:22.937 --> 00:10:24.793
"Mudbound" had not sold yet.

00:10:24.793 --> 00:10:28.380
My team I think had
taken a pass on it early,

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:29.987
and Charles called me up and said,

00:10:29.987 --> 00:10:34.050
"Ted, I know that you would get it.

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.320
Would you please do me a
favor and see the movie?"

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.470
And I said, "Look, there's
not even a screening, Charles.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:39.430
I can't even screen in anymore."

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.730
And he goes, "No, I'll get it to you,"

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:44.050
and brought over a
screener for "Mudbound."

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.350
I was able to watch it,
and the next morning,

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.020
I was at the breakfast table with Dee Rees

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.417
making the deal for "Mudbound,"

00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:52.330
and it did very well for us.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:54.510
We were very, very proud of that work.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.910
And you think about that little act

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:00.620
of Charles knowing to call me
and him being right about it,

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:01.663
which he always is,

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.340
and "Mudbound" became the
first Netflix original movie

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.663
to be nominated for an Academy Award.

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:10.860
So that's a pretty good endorsement.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.660
Tell me that story from your perspective.

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.380
It's a very, very real story.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:17.900
We knew we had a great film,

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.610
and where we were with the offers

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:24.190
from the other distributors was crickets.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:25.510
Things weren't looking good.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.605
And Ted, I just called them
up and said, "Hey, Ted, look,

00:11:28.605 --> 00:11:30.520
I don't reach out to
you on a lotta things,

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.460
but I think this is one that
will resonate with you."

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:34.963
And then the rest was history.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.130
But the other thing
that that movies showed

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.040
when they did share the data with us,

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.950
that movie was seen all around the world.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.960
At one point, I know
it was 25 million hours

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.750
of the film were viewed on their platform,

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:50.410
and they showed us a quick map of it.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:51.720
It was global.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.920
That notion that certain
content does not travel,

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.020
this is a story about a friendship

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:00.780
between a young African-American
and a young white guy,

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:04.130
shared connectivity, two
veterans coming back from war,

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.660
two families, black and white,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.440
just trying to live their lives, right?

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:10.070
Just basic universal themes,

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:12.020
and wanting better for their families,

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.410
and this notion that these
stories can't travel,

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.360
it was shown on that platform,

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:19.193
on that global platform,
it's just not true.

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:24.980
Macro stayed true to the principle

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.170
of having people of color

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.300
at the center of the creative process,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.700
and the work varies widely
in subject and tone.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.080
Soon after "Mudbound" came
"Sorry to Bother You,"

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.870
a darkly comedic, at
times fantastical movie

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:40.060
set in an alternative present day Oakland.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:43.530
King was also setting in
motion series like "Gentified."

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.430
That's about a trio of Latinx cousins

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.430
who save their grandfather's taco shop,

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.600
and it's currently in its
second season on Netflix.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.010
All of that success set up

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.757
what would become Macro's
defining project to date,

00:12:54.757 --> 00:12:56.757
"Judas and the Black Messiah."

00:12:56.757 --> 00:12:59.400
"Black Panther" director, Ryan
Coogler identified "Judas"

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.760
as the project he wanted to
produce after that blockbuster,

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.570
and teamed up with director Shaka King

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.240
to tell the true story of a radical leader

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.910
who was brought down by the FBI.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:11.079
Without even thinking about it,

00:13:11.079 --> 00:13:13.100
it was one of the easiest
decisions that I had ever made.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:14.970
It was just like, "Yo, we
gotta take this to Charles."

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:16.690
It's gotta be a MACRO project

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:20.750
so that we can walk into studios,

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.610
once we have a solid script,
we can walk into studios

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.690
with half the funding,

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:25.550
You know what I'm saying?

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.000
And just take some of the pressure off

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.440
'cause we know that the
pressure's gonna be there

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.640
when we say, "Look, we
making a period of film

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.840
about the Illinois chapter
of the Black Panther party.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.270
It's gonna be a predominantly Black cast."

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:41.160
We wanted to come in a position of power.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.321
You know what I mean?

00:13:42.321 --> 00:13:45.960
And Charles was the only
person we could go to for that.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.300
This is history that people need to know,

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.090
and the only way that we
can really heal as a country

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:54.340
is by recognizing past issues
and things that have happened,

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:57.880
and it was something that we
felt compelled to get behind.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:00.520
And yes, there was a level of risk to it,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.867
but then we also saw
the upside opportunity.

00:14:03.867 --> 00:14:07.751
And the Oscar goes to Daniel Kaluuya,

00:14:07.751 --> 00:14:09.830
"Judas and the Black Messiah."

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:13.100
I'd like to thank my
team, incredible support,

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:16.680
Charles King, man, everyone at Macro.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.100
And how much does the Oscar nominations

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:21.680
and all of that sort of award buzz do

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:24.890
for not just the film, but
candidly, for the company?

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:26.880
I mean, this feels like an inflection,

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:28.780
sort of catalytic moment for Macro.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:32.321
Yes, business-wise it's
absolutely, clearly

00:14:32.321 --> 00:14:33.900
is opening up more doors.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:35.700
We were already doing well.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.080
I mean, we've had 15
Academy Award nominations

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.230
for movies we've either
financed or produced,

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.910
but I'd say now, it's really
gonna be about continuing

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:45.050
that work, but also we're gonna expand.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.720
We're building a company that I believe,

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.220
within five to seven years from now,

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:52.170
will be a billion-plus,
diversified entertainment

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:53.070
and media company.

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.300
It's safe to say something
is changing in Hollywood,

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.370
from a more diverse
slate of Oscar nominees

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.760
to NBC ditching the Golden Globes,

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:06.470
to the commercial and critical success

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:09.100
of movies like "Judas"
and "One Night in Miami,"

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.390
not to mention the fierce anticipation

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:14.640
for "Black Panther"
sequel, "Wakanda Forever."

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.130
People of color finally
seem to be getting a seat

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.000
at the table in show business,

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.510
not just creatively, but on
the business side as well.

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:24.030
I'd say we're getting to the place

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:25.950
where we're within the system.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.500
I still don't look at
myself like we're one

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:31.900
of the establishment
or anything like that,

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.040
but I do think that by our success,

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:36.550
it absolutely inspired
and pushed the others

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:38.830
who have now taken the
entrepreneurial path.

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:41.520
And the thing about it is it's clear

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.120
that the content in this space
is resonating commercially.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.420
So many successes that we
can point to over the course

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.137
of the last few years from
"Hidden Figures, "Black Panther,"

00:15:50.137 --> 00:15:53.280
"Crazy Rich Asians,"
everything The Rock's in

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.820
because The Rock is a brother.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.790
He's black and Samoan, period.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:57.623
Right.

00:15:57.623 --> 00:16:00.120
So we know the value of our culture,

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:01.730
and now, we just need to be a part

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:03.840
of that value chain of ownership.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:04.800
Right.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:06.870
We're coming to the table to be partners

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.840
as opposed to saying please,
can you make this movie?

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:10.910
There's a difference in that,

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.370
and that absolutely
creates more opportunity

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.880
for those coming after
us and, in my opinion,

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.963
creates more opportunity for everyone.

